      <!-- WHICH COURT: MUSLIMS vs NON-MUSLIMS -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tracks-heading">
        <h2 id="tracks-heading">Which Law Applies to You: Muslim vs Non-Muslim</h2>

        <h3>For Muslim Couples</h3>
        <p>
          Muslim couples file at the Dubai Personal Status Court under <strong>Federal Decree-Law
          No. 41 of 2024</strong>. It applies to UAE citizens where one or both spouses are Muslim, and to
          foreigners unless one spouse insists on their own national law (Article 1). It also applies to
          divorce cases that had no final judgment when it came into force (Article 1(4)). The law covers
          talaq and its documentation, khula, judicial divorce for harm or discord, dowry, the waiting
          period (iddah), alimony and custody.
        </p>
        <p>
          Some points people often get wrong. A talaq repeated, or pronounced with a number such as
          "three", counts as one divorce (Article 57). A husband must document a divorce in court within
          15 days; if he does not, without an excuse the court accepts, the wife can claim compensation
          equal to alimony from the divorce to its documentation, and she keeps her right to file a case
          to prove the divorce (Article 58). Custody ends at 18 (Article 123),
          and from 15 a child may choose which parent to live with unless the child’s interest requires
          otherwise (Article 122).
        </p>

        <h3>For Non-Muslim Couples under Federal Decree-Law 41/2022</h3>
        <p>
          Non-Muslim couples use the same court building under <strong>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of
          2022</strong>. Key differences from the Muslim track:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><strong>No-fault divorce:</strong> either spouse’s wish to separate is enough; no need to show harm or blame (Articles 7 and 8)</li>
          <li><strong>No Family Guidance:</strong> divorce cases are not referred and go straight to court for a judgment at the first hearing (Article 3)</li>
          <li><strong>Served first:</strong> the divorce takes place by court decision after the other spouse is duly served (Article 8)</li>
          <li><strong>Alimony after the judgment:</strong> the wife applies after the divorce judgment, and the judge decides using listed factors such as the years of marriage, her age and the financial position as assessed by an accounting expert (Article 9)</li>
          <li><strong>Joint custody:</strong> custody is a joint and equal right of both parents (Article 10) until the child is 18 (Article 4)</li>
          <li><strong>No automatic equal split:</strong> the law has no rule that divides property in half</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If one spouse is a foreigner who holds to the law of their home country, that law may apply
          instead (Article 1 of both laws). Mixed couples, where one spouse is Muslim, should confirm which
          law applies before filing. Our <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">non-Muslim divorce guide</a>
          covers the civil track in more depth.
        </p>

        <h3>What About the DIFC Courts?</h3>
        <p>
          Some websites describe the DIFC Courts as a "third option" for non-Muslim divorce in Dubai.
          They are not. The DIFC Courts are the English-language common-law courts of the Dubai
          International Financial Centre, and they have no family jurisdiction:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>They cannot grant a divorce decree</li>
          <li>They do not hear custody, alimony or child maintenance cases</li>
          <li>They do not decide financial claims between spouses, even when assets or companies sit inside the DIFC</li>
          <li>There is no DIFC filing fee for a divorce, because there is no DIFC divorce filing</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A non-Muslim divorce in Dubai goes to the Personal Status Court under Decree-Law 41/2022. Our guide
          to <a href="/en/difc-divorce-dubai/">why the DIFC Courts cannot grant a divorce</a> explains
          the difference in more detail.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Not sure which track applies?</strong> The choice between the Muslim and non-Muslim
          tracks, or a home-country law, and the question of which emirate’s court can hear your case,
          change what you can claim. A short consultation with a UAE-licensed family lawyer before filing
          helps you start in the right place.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- FAMILY GUIDANCE SECTION -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="guidance-heading">
        <h2 id="guidance-heading">Family Conciliation (Family Guidance): Is It Required?</h2>
        <p>
          Dubai Courts calls the service <strong>Family Conciliation</strong>; the 2024 law calls it
          Family Guidance. Under Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, before a case goes to
          court the supervising judge <strong>may</strong> refer the spouses to Family Guidance to settle
          amicably, if he or she considers it feasible. It is not automatic.
        </p>

        <h3>Matters That Are Not Referred</h3>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>Wills and inheritance</li>
          <li>Urgent and temporary cases, including urgent or temporary orders on alimony, custody and tutorship</li>
          <li>Cases where reconciliation is not foreseeable, such as cases to prove a marriage or to prove a divorce</li>
          <li>Non-Muslim divorces under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, which are never referred (Article 3)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How the Dubai Courts Service Works</h3>
        <p>
          According to the Dubai Courts service page, Family Conciliation is open to citizens, residents
          and people who work in Dubai. You apply on the Dubai Courts website (after registering) or at a
          government service centre, upload the marriage contract as a PDF, and pay no fee. A file ends in
          one of four ways: a settlement, a waiver, closure of the file, or referral to court.
        </p>
        <p>
          A settlement reached in Family Guidance is recorded in a report signed by both spouses and the
          counsellor and approved by the supervising judge. It then has the force of an execution writ and
          cannot be appealed unless it contradicts the law (Article 8(2)). Read every term before you sign.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Dubai Courts Personal Status Claims service asks for the Family Conciliation referral
          alongside the statement of claim, so expect to start with conciliation unless your matter is
          exempt. If it is exempt, ask the call centre or an Al Adheed centre what to upload instead.
        </p>

        <h3>No Fixed Family Guidance Period</h3>
        <p>
          The law sets no time limit for the guidance stage. The 60 days often quoted belongs to a later
          stage: in contested divorces for harm or discord, the two arbitrators the court appoints have up
          to <strong>60 days from their appointment</strong> (Article 72), down from 90 under the old law.
        </p>

        <h3>Practical Tips for the Sessions</h3>
        <p>
          What you say about money, the children or the reasons for the breakdown can shape how you
          approach the court stage, so think it through before you attend. Counsellors are not advocates
          for either spouse. A clear, calm account of your situation and a realistic view of what you want
          for the children tend to help more than a list of grievances. For a fuller walk-through of this
          stage, see our guide to the <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">Family Guidance Section in
          the UAE</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- STEP BY STEP FILING -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="filing-heading">
        <h2 id="filing-heading">Step by Step: Filing a Case at Dubai Personal Status Court</h2>
        <p>
          This is the general route for a Dubai personal status claim, based on the Dubai Courts service
          pages and the Civil Procedure Code (Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022). Non-Muslim divorces skip
          Step 2. For the divorce-specific detail, see our guide on
          <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file for divorce in Dubai</a>.
        </p>

        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <h3>Step 1: Confirm Dubai Is the Right Court and Which Law Applies</h3>
            <p>
              Check the jurisdiction rules in Article 3 of the 2024 law (where each of you lives or works,
              and where the marital home is) and which law covers you under Article 1 of the 2024 and 2022
              laws. The Dubai Courts claim service expects one party to be a citizen or resident of Dubai.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 2: Family Conciliation, If Your Matter Goes There</h3>
            <p>
              Apply online or at a government service centre with the marriage contract as a PDF. There
              is no fee. The file ends in a settlement, a waiver, closure, or referral to court. Keep the
              referral, because the claim service asks for it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 3: Prepare Your Documents</h3>
            <p>
              Scan everything as PDFs. Anything not in Arabic needs a legal Arabic translation, and foreign
              documents must be legalised first (see the documents table below).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 4: Register the Claim</h3>
            <p>
              Upload the statement of claim and the Family Conciliation referral through the Dubai Courts
              website, signing in with UAE PASS, or register at an Al Adheed centre. The Civil Procedure
              Code allows a statement of claim in electronic or paper form under the court’s procedures
              (Article 44(1)). If you have no domicile in the UAE, you must name an address for service in
              the UAE and a legal representative (Article 44(2)(a)).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 5: Pay the Fee on Time</h3>
            <p>
              The personal status fee is <strong>AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000</strong>
              (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015). Pay within 3 business days, counted from the day after the payment
              notice, or the registration is void (Article 47(1) of the Civil Procedure Code). See our
              breakdown of <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">divorce costs in the UAE</a> for translation and
              lawyer costs on top.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 6: The Court Serves the Other Party</h3>
            <p>
              Service can be made by recorded audio or video call, SMS, smart applications, email or fax,
              or by hand at the home, workplace or chosen address; failing that, by posting on the court’s
              website (Article 9 of the Civil Procedure Code). Electronic service counts from the date it is sent (Article 11). The
              defendant has 10 business days from service to file a defence (Article 48(2)).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 7: Hearings and Judgment</h3>
            <p>
              The judge can deal with urgent matters while the case runs; for example, during an alimony
              case the court may order temporary alimony for the wife and children at her request, and that
              order is enforceable immediately (Article 100 of the 2024 law). In contested discord cases
              the court appoints two arbitrators, who have up to 60 days (Article 72). A non-Muslim civil
              divorce is decided at the first hearing (Article 3 of the 2022 law). Dubai Courts issues the
              first-instance judgment electronically. How long all this takes depends on what is disputed;
              see our <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/">UAE divorce timeline guide</a> and the guide to
              <a href="/en/contested-vs-uncontested-divorce-uae/">contested vs uncontested divorce</a>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Step 8: Appeal or Enforce</h3>
            <p>
              Either side has <strong>30 days</strong> to appeal under Article 9 of the 2024 law, counted
              from the day after the judgment if they attended, otherwise from the day after notice
              (Article 10). If the other side does not pay what the judgment orders, you open an enforcement
              file (AED 50 under Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015); ask at the Personal Status Court which office
              handles enforcement of your judgment.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- SERVICES AND FEES -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="smart-heading">
        <h2 id="smart-heading">Dubai Courts Services and Fees for a Personal Status Case</h2>
        <p>
          These are the Dubai Courts services you are most likely to meet in a family file, with the fees
          set by Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 Concerning Judicial Fees of the Dubai Courts and the Dubai Courts
          service pages.
        </p>
        <div class="doc-table-wrapper">
          <table class="doc-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Service</th><th>Used for</th><th>Fee</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Family Conciliation</td>
                <td>Trying to settle a family dispute before it reaches court; online or at a government service centre</td>
                <td>No fee</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Personal Status Claims</td>
                <td>Filing a statement of claim; online or at an Al Adheed centre</td>
                <td>AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Documentations and Ishhadat</td>
                <td>Formal documentation before the court; in person or through an agent with a special official power of attorney, with original ID and two witnesses</td>
                <td>AED 220</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Order on petition or travel ban request</td>
                <td>Court permission where the law requires it, and urgent orders</td>
                <td>AED 100</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Appeal</td>
                <td>Challenging a first-instance judgment</td>
                <td>AED 100 (alimony appeals free)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Cassation</td>
                <td>Challenging an appeal judgment on the law</td>
                <td>AED 2,000</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Enforcement file</td>
                <td>Enforcing a judgment, such as unpaid alimony</td>
                <td>AED 50</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">
          Fees change from time to time; confirm the current amount when you register. Translation,
          legalisation and lawyer fees are separate.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the law requires court permission, you apply by "order on petition" to the court where you
          live, and any interested party may appeal the order within one week (Article 6 of the 2024 law).
          A travel ban request carries the same AED 100 fee; see our guide to a
          <a href="/en/travel-ban-divorce-uae/">travel ban during a divorce</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- DOCUMENTS -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="docs-heading">
        <h2 id="docs-heading">Documents Required to File at Dubai Personal Status Court</h2>
        <p>
          Incomplete files and untranslated or unlegalised foreign papers slow a case down. Dubai Courts
          asks for documents in PDF with a legal Arabic translation, and foreign documents must be
          legalised before they are translated and used.
        </p>
        <div class="doc-table-wrapper">
          <table class="doc-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Document</th><th>Notes</th><th>Legalisation needed?</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Statement of claim</td>
                <td>Uploaded as a PDF when you register the claim</td>
                <td>No</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Family Conciliation referral</td>
                <td>Uploaded with the claim where the matter went through conciliation</td>
                <td>No (issued by Dubai Courts)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Marriage contract or certificate</td>
                <td>PDF, with a legal Arabic translation if it is not in Arabic</td>
                <td>Yes, if issued abroad</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Emirates IDs and passports</td>
                <td>Original ID is required for in-person documentation services</td>
                <td>No</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Children’s birth certificates</td>
                <td>Needed where custody, visitation or child maintenance is claimed</td>
                <td>Yes, if issued abroad</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Proof of income and expenses</td>
                <td>Salary certificate, bank statements, rent and school fees; alimony is set by the payer’s means and the recipient’s needs (Article 96)</td>
                <td>Translation if not in Arabic</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Power of attorney</td>
                <td>If a lawyer or agent acts for you</td>
                <td>Yes, if signed abroad</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">
          Legalisation chain for foreign documents: the issuing country’s foreign ministry, then the UAE
          embassy in that country, then the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs, followed by a legal Arabic
          translation.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a fuller checklist by case type, see our guide to the
          <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents required for divorce in the UAE</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ONLINE FILING -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="online-heading">
        <h2 id="online-heading">Filing Online and Attending Remotely</h2>
        <p>
          You do not have to visit Al Garhoud to start a case. Dubai Courts lists its website and the
          Al Adheed centres as channels for personal status claims, and Family Conciliation can be
          requested on the website after registering. Individuals sign in to Dubai Courts e-services with
          UAE PASS; confirm the current sign-in options on the Dubai Courts website.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Civil Procedure Code backs this up. Fee collection, registration, service, documents,
          attendance, pleading, witnesses, judgments, appeals and execution are all valid if done wholly or
          partly remotely (Article 330), and remote proceedings can reach a person outside the emirate in
          coordination with the local authority (Article 332). At each remote hearing any party may ask the
          court to hold the trial in person, and the court decides after notifying the others
          (Article 333). An enforceable copy of a judgment obtained remotely carries the writ of execution
          and an electronic signature (Article 131(1)).
        </p>
        <p>
          If you live abroad, you need an address for service in the UAE and a legal representative
          (Article 44(2)(a)), and a power of attorney signed abroad must be legalised before it can be used.
          Our guide to <a href="/en/online-divorce-uae/">online divorce in the UAE</a> walks through filing,
          attending and tracking a case from a screen.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FIRST DAY -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="firstday-heading">
        <h2 id="firstday-heading">What to Bring on the Day</h2>
        <p>
          Check the address on your notice first: personal status cases are at Al Garhoud, not the main
          building in Umm Hurair 2. Confirm the hours, especially in Ramadan
          or near a public holiday, and dress as you would for any government office.
        </p>
        <p>
          What to carry:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>Your original Emirates ID and passport, plus copies</li>
          <li>Your case number, hearing notice or application reference</li>
          <li>The originals of the documents you uploaded (marriage contract, children’s birth certificates) with their legal Arabic translations</li>
          <li>Your Family Conciliation referral or settlement report, if you have one</li>
          <li>Fee receipts</li>
          <li>For a documentation (Ishhadat) appointment: original ID and two witnesses, or an agent with a special official power of attorney</li>
          <li>Financial papers (salary certificate, recent bank statements, rent and school fees) if money is claimed</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Court papers are in Arabic, which is why Dubai Courts asks for a legal Arabic translation of
          everything else. If you do not read Arabic and have no lawyer, bring someone who does. Keep every receipt and stamped page, as you will need them later. Expats often
          ask whether filing in another emirate would be quicker; our comparison of
          <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai versus Abu Dhabi divorce</a> explains why the
          jurisdiction rules, not convenience, decide where you file.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- COURT HIERARCHY / APPEALS -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="hierarchy-heading">
        <h2 id="hierarchy-heading">Appeals: Court of First Instance, Appeal and Cassation</h2>
        <p>
          Dubai has its own court system with three levels. The level your case has reached decides what
          you can still challenge and how quickly you must act.
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <h3>Court of First Instance (Personal Status)</h3>
            <p>
              Every case starts here. The judge hears the parties, receives documents, can appoint
              experts or arbitrators, and issues the first-instance judgment, which Dubai Courts delivers
              electronically.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Court of Appeal</h3>
            <p>
              Under the 2024 law either party has <strong>30 days</strong> to appeal (Article 9), counted
              from the day after the judgment if they attended, or from the day after notice if they did
              not (Article 10). The appeal fee is AED 100, and appeals in alimony cases are free. Our
              guide on <a href="/en/divorce-appeal-uae/">appealing a divorce decision in the UAE</a> covers
              grounds and preparation.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Court of Cassation</h3>
            <p>
              The highest court in Dubai, which reviews how the law was applied rather than rehearing the
              facts. The same 30-day period applies to cassation in personal status matters (Article 9),
              and the fee is AED 2,000. Not every judgment can go to cassation, so ask a lawyer early
              whether yours can.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Two limits to know. A settlement approved by the judge after Family Guidance cannot be appealed
          unless it contradicts the law (Article 8(2)). Non-Muslim civil cases have their own rules on what
          can be appealed, so check the position on the day you receive the judgment.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- REPRESENTATION -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="rep-heading">
        <h2 id="rep-heading">Do You Need a Lawyer at Dubai Personal Status Court?</h2>
        <p>
          You can appear yourself. Article 52(1) of the Civil Procedure Code lets litigants appear in
          person or through an attorney: a lawyer, or a relative or in-law up to the fourth degree.
          Whether you should depends on the case. Points to weigh:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li>The court works in <strong>Arabic</strong>, and Dubai Courts asks for a legal Arabic translation of your documents</li>
          <li>Deadlines are short: 3 business days to pay the fee after the payment notice, 10 business days for a defence, 30 days to appeal</li>
          <li>Money claims have time limits: a wife’s past maintenance is not heard for more than two years before filing (Article 99(3)), and a child’s past maintenance from the father for more than one year (Article 111)</li>
          <li>A settlement approved by the judge is enforceable and generally cannot be appealed (Article 8(2))</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In Dubai, the Government of Dubai Legal Affairs Department keeps separate rolls for advocates and
          legal consultants. Before you instruct anyone, check that the person who will appear for you is
          on the advocates’ roll, and ask for the fee agreement in writing. Lawyer fees vary with the case;
          see our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-dubai/">divorce lawyers in Dubai</a> for what to
          check and what to ask.
        </p>

        <div class="cta-inline">
          <p>Ask to be matched with a UAE-licensed family lawyer.</p>
          <a href="#contact" class="btn btn--primary">Free Legal Consultation</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- AMICABLE ROUTE -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="amicable-heading">
        <h2 id="amicable-heading">Settling: The Agreed Route Through the Court</h2>
        <p>
          If you and your spouse agree on the divorce and on money and children, a written settlement
          narrows what the judge has to decide. A settlement reached in Family Guidance is signed by both
          spouses and the counsellor, approved by the supervising judge, and then has the force of an
          execution writ (Article 8(2)).
        </p>
        <p>
          On the Muslim track a wife can also ask for khula, where the husband accepts a consideration; if
          he stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula for an appropriate consideration, and the
          consideration can never waive the children’s rights, maintenance or custody (Article 66). On the
          civil track either spouse can file without giving a reason, and the divorce is decided at the
          first hearing (Articles 3 and 7 of the 2022 law). Read more in our guide to
          <a href="/en/mutual-consent-divorce-uae/">mutual consent divorce in the UAE</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even in an agreed case, have a lawyer read the settlement before you sign it. Once the judge
          approves it, a term you regret, particularly on deferred dowry or child maintenance, is hard to
          undo.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- DUBAI vs ABU DHABI -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="comparison-heading">
        <h2 id="comparison-heading">Dubai Personal Status Court vs Abu Dhabi: Key Differences</h2>
        <p>
          Where you file is decided by the jurisdiction rules, not by which court looks quicker. Under
          Article 3 of the 2024 law the competent court is normally where the respondent lives or works;
          for divorce, alimony, custody and dowry claims by a wife, children, parents or a custodian, the
          plaintiff’s place and the marital home also count. If you could file in either emirate, the
          differences below matter.
        </p>

        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h3>Dubai Personal Status Court</h3>
            <ul class="list">
              <li>Muslims: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024; Family Guidance at the judge’s discretion (Article 8)</li>
              <li>Non-Muslims: same court, Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022; no Family Guidance; divorce decided at the first hearing (Article 3)</li>
              <li>Fee: AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000 (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015)</li>
              <li>Filing: Dubai Courts website with UAE PASS, or an Al Adheed centre</li>
              <li>Non-Muslim custody: joint and equal until the child is 18</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h3>Abu Dhabi Courts</h3>
            <ul class="list">
              <li>Muslims: Abu Dhabi Personal Status Court under the same federal 2024 law</li>
              <li>Non-Muslims: Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 and its 2022 Regulation, with English-language proceedings</li>
              <li>Civil divorce application fee: <strong>AED 5,000</strong></li>
              <li>The hearing is set at least 30 days after the application is approved, and the divorce is decided at that first hearing</li>
              <li>Joint custody until 16, then the child chooses (2022 Regulation, Article 25)</li>
              <li>The ADGM Courts have no family jurisdiction, so they cannot grant a divorce either</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Filing in an emirate whose courts have no jurisdiction over your case only adds delay. Our
          <a href="/en/family-court-abu-dhabi/">Abu Dhabi family court guide</a> explains which Abu Dhabi
          court hears which case, its addresses and its fees.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2024 LAW CHANGES -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="law2024-heading">
        <h2 id="law2024-heading">What the 2024 Muslim Personal Status Law Changed at the Dubai Court</h2>
        <p>
          Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 replaced the older personal status statute and has applied
          since 15 April 2025, including to divorce cases that had no final judgment by then
          (Article 1(4)). For Muslim couples at the Dubai Personal Status Court, the changes people notice
          most:
        </p>
        <ul class="list">
          <li><strong>Family Guidance is discretionary:</strong> the supervising judge may refer a case if a settlement seems feasible, and urgent matters, inheritance and cases to prove a marriage or divorce are exempt (Article 8)</li>
          <li><strong>Arbitration has a 60-day cap:</strong> court-appointed arbitrators in contested discord cases have up to 60 days from appointment, down from 90 (Article 72)</li>
          <li><strong>Talaq must be documented within 15 days:</strong> documenting is a duty, and a husband who is late without an excuse the court accepts owes compensation equal to alimony up to the date of documentation (Article 58)</li>
          <li><strong>Repeated talaq counts once:</strong> a divorce repeated or coupled with a number, spoken, written or by signal, counts as one (Article 57)</li>
          <li><strong>Custody runs to 18:</strong> custody ends at 18 (Article 123), and from 15 a child may choose which parent to live with (Article 122)</li>
          <li><strong>30 days to appeal:</strong> the appeal and cassation period in personal status matters is 30 days (Article 9)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          See our guide to the <a href="/en/new-uae-personal-status-law-2025/">new UAE Personal Status
          Law</a> for the full list of changes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- RELATED LINKS -->
      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
        <h2 id="related-heading">Related Guides</h2>
        <div class="related-links">
          <a href="/en/divorce-timeline-uae/" class="related-link">
